.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-volume-compact 1  "tapectl-volume-compact " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-volume\-compact \- Interactive compaction: read + write + finish in one flow
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl volume compact\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-to\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-missing\-escrow\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-full\-confirm\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fILABEL\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Interactive compaction: read + write + finish in one flow
.PP
One drive (`\-\-device`) serves both the read and the write, so the flow PAUSES after step 1 for you to unload the source and load the destination — always, with or without `\-\-to`. It therefore needs a terminal, and without one it refuses before reading anything. To compact unattended, run the steps separately: `volume compact\-read <SOURCE>`, swap cartridges, `volume compact\-write \-\-to <DEST>`, then `volume compact\-finish <SOURCE>`.
.PP
Step 3 applies `compact\-finish`\*(Aqs ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 gate and may refuse non\-interactively without `\-\-force`. When it does, the destination tape is already written and sealed and nothing is lost: `volume compact\-finish <SOURCE> \-\-force` completes the flow without re\-reading or re\-writing anything.
.PP
Exit status: `volume write`\*(Aqs, for step 2\*(Aqs write (0 sealed and confirmed, 2 nothing written, 3 confirm inconclusive, 4 interrupted, 5 aborted, 6 quarantined, 75 catalog busy). A step 1 failure exits 2: step 1 writes nothing. Step 3 refused for consent (no terminal, no \-\-yes) exits 0 with a warning naming `volume compact\-finish \-\-force`: the destination is sealed, confirmed and a full carry\-forward. Step 3 refused because content was not carried forward exits 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fI<TO>\fR
Destination volume label (already initialised).

With it, the pause after step 1 only asks you to swap cartridges and press Enter; without it, that pause also asks for the label. It never skips the swap: the destination is written through the same drive the source was read from.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path).

Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-missing\-escrow\fR
See `volume write \-\-allow\-missing\-escrow`
.TP
\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR
See `volume write \-\-prewrite\-hash` (step 2\*(Aqs write)
.TP
\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR \fI<FILL_CEILING>\fR
See `volume write \-\-fill\-ceiling` (step 2\*(Aqs write)
.TP
\fB\-\-full\-confirm\fR
See `volume write \-\-full\-confirm` (step 2\*(Aqs write)
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Waive step 3\*(Aqs Tier\-2 prompt, as `volume compact\-finish \-\-force`.

See `volume compact\-finish \-\-force`: step 3\*(Aqs ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 gate.

With this (or the global `\-\-yes`) step 3 asks nothing; the cartridge swap after step 1 still waits for you.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fILABEL\fR>
Source volume label
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
